The vector pointing from $(2,3,4)$ to $(4 - 2t, 6t, 1 - 3t)$ is then
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 2t \\ -3 + 6t \\ -3 - 3t \end{pmatrix}.\]For the point on the line that is closest to $(2,3,4),$ this vector will be orthogonal to the direction vector of the second line, which is $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.$  Thus,
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 2t \\ -3 + 6t \\ -3 - 3t \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = 0.\]This gives us $(2 - 2t)(-2) + (-3 + 6t)(6) + (-3 - 3t)(-3) = 0.$  Solving, we find $t = \frac{13}{49}.$

For this value of $t,$ the point is $\boxed{\left( \frac{170}{49}, \frac{78}{49}, \frac{10}{49} \right)}.$

response:
 Note that the direction vector of the line is $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$. A normal to this direction vector is $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$. Then the point on the line that is closest to the point $(2, 3, 4)$ lies on a line through $(2, 3, 4)$ that is orthogonal to the given line. This line has direction vector $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$. Then this line passes through the point
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} + s \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3s + 2 \\ s + 3 \\ 2s + 4 \end{pmatrix}\]for some value of $s$. Furthermore, this is the same as the point that is being sought, so we can write
\[\begin{pmatrix} 3s + 2 \\ s + 3 \\ 2s + 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.\]We solve for $s$ and $t$. Setting the $\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$-coordinates to be equal, we get
\[3s + 2 = 4 - 2s \quad \text{and} \quad s + 3 = 6s - 3t.\]Solving, we find $s = 2/5$, and hence $t = 8/15$.

